    "title": "GOLMET CLI — release v1.7.4",
    "text": "GOLMET CLI — release v1.7.4 <p align=\"center\" English | <a href=\"/docs/readme.pt-br\" Português Brasil </a </p <p align=\"center\" <a href=\"https://github.com/Golmet\" </a </p <h1 align=\"center\" golmet-cli</h1 <p align=\"center\" <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/golmet-cli\" <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/golmet-cli.svg\" alt=\"npm version\" </a <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/golmet-cli\" <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/dw/golmet-cli.svg\" alt=\"npm downloads\" </a <a href=\"https://github.com/Golmet/golmet-site/blob/main/LICENSE\" <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg\" alt=\"MIT License\" </a </p The problem Golmet solves Your requirements live in a document that nobody re-reads. The specs became a diagram drawn at the start of the project and never updated again. The code ships however it can, and when someone asks \"why did this feature ship like this?\", the answer is \"ask the analyst, they decided — I think\". Three months later, even the analyst doesn't remember. The worst part isn't losing the requirement. It's that nobody trusts the documentation anymore — it's always stale, always incomplete, always somewhere else. So the team stops writing it, and the knowledge leaves when the person leaves. Golmet exists for that: to make documentation the trustworthy part of the project again — because it is machine-verified against what the code actually does, on every validation, before any merge. What Golmet does, in practice A real journey, from zero to delivery. The analyst opens the company folder and runs golmet init : the Organization is born — the golmet.yaml that declares the products, the constitution, the index. Inside it he creates the product's project, and the project is born with the right documents already in place: the PROJECT_CHARTER.md what the product is , the REQUIREMENTS.md the business rules, one per section, with state and owner . Not every project starts from zero. When the system already exists - in production, with history - the journey starts with reverse engineering : GOLMET freezes the source and turns it into documented truth models, contracts, business rules - every claim with its file, line and hash , seeds the candidate queue and hands the human the guided reading of the legacy. The new workflow is planned on documented truth, not on the model's memory. See reverse engineering docs/concepts/reverse.md . When the client asks for the feature \"booking with slot confirmation\", the requirement enters REQUIREMENTS as a RN-001 — ... section — and each rule carries evidence : the file, the line and the hash of the code that implements it. The technical spec descends into specs/ , the task into delivery/ , the code ships. When anyone runs golmet validate , the kit checks: does rule RN-001 point at code that exists? Does the hash match the HEAD? Is the spec stale? What diverged shows up — and doesn't merge. When the project ends, what remains is not a forgotten Word file: it's a knowledge package — the norms of how that organization works, the patterns its projects revealed, the researched domain foundation. The next project starts with all of it already loaded. AI through the whole cycle. Golmet is not a lonely validator: it ships with a workflow of gm- skills your AI executes - the AI elicits the requirements with you , the AI writes the spec, the AI writes the code with green tests against the approved spec, the AI reviews the code against the spec. Documentation stops being the thing nobody has time for. The recommended mode To get the most out of Golmet: Organization + FULL profile + Opencode. It is the tested path and the one this guide follows — the Organization gives you the structure and the shared knowledge, the FULL profile installs the complete workflow into your engine, and Opencode is the engine where the kit's capabilities run without reduction. Other engines work with the essential profile, the kit lives versioned in the repo — but it is this mode where nothing is missing. Details in docs/recommended.md docs/recommended.md . Getting started 5 minutes Updating by channel: golmet upgrade self-update updates Go/binary installations; via npm, update with npm update -g golmet-cli . The documentation The public docs are written as a journey — start there: | Doc | What | | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | | docs/getting-started/ docs/getting-started/ | Start here. Why Golmet exists, and the journey: the Organization, the project, working with AI, the cycle of one feature, what remains at the end | | docs/concepts/overview.md docs/concepts/overview.md | What GOLMET delivers — the full map in one page: everything, layer by layer the tutorials show the journey; this is the map | | docs/concepts/artefatos.md docs/concepts/artefatos.md | The artifact architecture: what each document is for, what feeds it, what it feeds | | docs/recommended.md docs/recommended.md | The recommended mode Organization + FULL + Opencode — and what you lose without it | | docs/concepts/ docs/concepts/ | The concepts in depth: organization and versioning, content layers, profile, knowledge, self-governing | | docs/FORMAT.md docs/FORMAT.md | The normative spec v1 — the contract every check enforces | | docs/reference/compatibility.md docs/reference/compatibility.md | The real MCP x engine compatibility matrix - verified cell by cell, locked by tests honest \"unverified\" marks | | docs/reference/ docs/reference/ | Reference: the 18 commands, the 14 MCP tools | | docs/reference/lint.md docs/reference/lint.md | The lint layers in depth: what each check L1-L14 validates, why it exists, its finding example and the test that locks it | | CHANGELOG.md CHANGELOG.md | Wave by wave, with the real-world motivation | | LICENSE LICENSE | MIT | The public site renders this documentation tree after the release — the repo is the source, the site is a render. Uninstall golmet uninstall removes the global Golmet structure layer by layer, with dynamic discovery of the real state manifest + personal.json + engine config . Everything is selected by default EXCEPT packs — the pack's remote repository is never touched. Flags: --only <csv --yes non-interactive · --dry-run · --json . Development The CLI is written in Go 1.22+ module github.com/Golmet/golmet-cli , with spf13/cobra https://github.com/spf13/cobra as the only external dependency. 639 Go tests unit + integration + binary e2e back the current state. Roadmap · Contributing · License The milestone plan lives in ROADMAP.md ROADMAP.md F1 = this release; F2 = the docs site; F3 = the contribute flow and workspace migration . Contributing: CONTRIBUTING.md CONTRIBUTING.md — table-driven tests and gofmt are mandatory. License MIT LICENSE ."
